<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Push-capable release manifest (Android Repair 07).

      PUSH ONLY. This file grants the notification permission and nothing
      else. It is NOT part of any ordinary build: android/app/build.gradle
      substitutes it for src/release/AndroidManifest.xml when
      pushNativeCapabilityMaterialized is true and
      voiceNativeCapabilityMaterialized is false.

      pushNativeCapabilityMaterialized is the native projection of the SAME
      decision Repair 05 already uses for runtime activation --
      remotePushCapabilityEnabled, derived from EXPO_PUBLIC_SMART_WATCHLIST_V1
      and nothing else. It is deliberately NOT keyed on
      EAS_BUILD_PROFILE == staging-certification (a future production profile
      may legitimately enable push) and NOT on the presence of
      GOOGLE_SERVICES_JSON (a credential is configuration, not product
      availability).

      INDEPENDENT OF VOICE. The Voice selector never selects this file and
      this selector never selects a Voice manifest. A build with Voice on and
      push off gets src/certification, which grants no notification
      permission; a build with both gets src/voicePush. Certification happens
      to enable both capabilities today, but that coincidence is not the
      architecture.

      WHY A PROFILE-SPECIFIC MANIFEST INSTEAD OF app.json:
      Android is NATIVE_AUTHORITATIVE (config/native-config-authority.json),
      so app.json cannot express a permission that exists in one build
      configuration only. Granting POST_NOTIFICATIONS in app.json + src/main
      would broaden the ORDINARY PRODUCTION artifact, which ships no remote-push
      consumer at all. app.json therefore keeps POST_NOTIFICATIONS in
      android.blockedPermissions -- which remains true of the default artifact --
      and the grant lives only here. scripts/check-native-config-parity.js
      enforces exactly that relationship.

      WHAT THIS FILE MUST NEVER CONTAIN: a <service> element of any kind, or
      any re-grant of the foreground-service permissions Repair 02 removed. A
      notification-capable build is precisely the shape that attracts a media
      or foreground-service contribution, so both are named below and are
      declared in this exception's mustRemainRemovedEverywhere.
    -->

    <!-- Carried verbatim from src/release/AndroidManifest.xml. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW" tools:node="remove"/>

    <!--
      The push grant. tools:node="replace" supersedes the tools:node="remove"
      that src/main/AndroidManifest.xml applies to this same permission: a
      build-type source set outranks main in the manifest merger, exactly as
      src/certification's RECORD_AUDIO grant does for Voice.

      Artifact-verified claim: confirm against the merged manifest of the built
      AAB before shipping a push-capable artifact.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" tools:node="replace"/>

    <!--
      Repair 02 foreground-service removals, restated for this variant.
      src/main already removes both and an overlay only adds, so these are
      belt-and-braces -- but naming them here means a library that later
      contributes one cannot slip it into a push-capable artifact unnoticed,
      and the parity gate enforces their presence.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" tools:node="remove"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK" tools:node="remove"/>

</manifest>
